/T (TOPIC A)
/E (TOPIC A)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 393 0 R
/T (Measures to strengthen the regulation and traceability of wildlife trade and its biological derivatives. )
/E (Measures to strengthen the regulation and traceability of wildlife trade and its biological derivatives. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E ( Unregulated wildlife trade represents the commercialization and markets of wild species, or their parts, in ways that infringe legal frameworks, and bypass international sustainability safeguards. This includes the trade of live animals, as well as derived products such as skins, scales, ivory, and other biological materials. Through this practice, all these can travel through complex supply chains across international borders.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E ( A key challenge in addressing this issue is traceability, referring to the ability to verify and keep record of where a specimen came from, how it moved through the supply chain, and whether it was purchased legally. Traceability relies on multiple systems and tools, such as species identifications and standardized tracking mechanisms that allow authorities to distinguish legal trade from illegal commerce and laundering.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E ( Traceability also implies more than identifying a species at a single checkpoint. A traceable wildlife product should provide a complete history of its origin, handling, and movement from harvest to final sale. This matters because unregulated trade does not only involve live animals or exotic pets; sectors such as fisheries, timber, and wild plants are also especially at risk. Effective traceability systems in these industries can support species conservation while also protecting the livelihoods of communities that depend on these resources. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E ( A major reason traceability remains difficult is that wildlife products often move through multiple intermediaries and jurisdictions, which means regulation depends on consistent documentation, verification, and information-sharing across borders. In the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora \(CITES\), the permit system is described as the backbone of regulated international trade in listed species, but when permits and records are incomplete, inconsistent, or difficult to authenticate, illegal products can be laundered into legal channels. To address these gaps, CITES has promoted electronic permitting \(eCITES\), which supports electronic application and issuance, permit authentication, and standardized reporting tools that can strengthen transparency and reduce opportunities for fraud. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 393 0 R
/T (Introduction to the topic)
/E (Introduction to the topic)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 393 0 R
/Pg 20 0 R
/A [1739 0 R 0]
/K [9]
