endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 394 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [1740 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 394 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [1741 0 R 0]
/K [1]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E ( Despite receiving less public attention than other international crimes, the scale of this issue makes the topic incredibly urgent. Between 2015 and 2021, illegal trade across 162 countries involved roughly 4,000 species, and approximately 13 million items. This is clear evidence of regulations and enforcement challenges that affect biodiversity and the survival of threatened species.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E ( As an organization primarily focused on conservation and protection of wildlife, WWF is at a key position regarding the international response to environmental pressures affecting species and ecosystems. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 394 0 R
/T (Historical Context and Background)
/E (Historical Context and Background)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E (  Many of the current-day discussions regarding the aforementioned wildlife traceability back to historical tensions between conservation needs, financial systems, and law enforcement. As a key example, Kenya is often mentioned for a defining moment in 1989, when the president burned 12 tons of confiscated ivory. This act is described as a strong factor for the international ivory trade ban that happened in that same year. It also reaffirmed the country's stance against reopening ivory or rhino horn trade.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E ( As international trade expanded, conservation concerns increasingly required cross-border regulation rather than isolated national bans. A major turning point was the creation of CITES \(Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora\). This document was drafted following an IUCN resolution in the 1960s, adopted in 1973, and entered into force in 1975. CITES was designed specifically to ensure that international trade in wild animals and plants does not threaten species survival, recognizing that international cooperation is essential when trade chains cross multiple jurisdictions.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E ( CITES operationalized regulation through a system of permits and certificates required for import, export, and re-export of listed species and their parts and derivatives. This documentation framework became a central international model for traceability because it relies on standardized records as a condition for legal movement across borders. In other words, legal wildlife trade increasingly depended on paperwork and verification processes that could be checked at borders. This created a clear distinction between regulated trade and illicit flows. Over time, however, the effectiveness of this approach depended heavily on consistent implementation and verification capacity across countries.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E ( As trade volume and complexity grew, paper-based systems also faced new pressures, including authentication challenges and slower information-sharing between authorities. In response, CITES began promoting the previously mentioned electronic permitting \(eCITES\), which includes electronic application and issuance of permits, permit control and authentication, and electronic reporting. Early steps included pilot efforts \(notably involving Switzerland and the United Kingdom\) launched around 2009, aimed at exchanging permit information electronically. This )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
