/P 396 0 R
/E ( Multiple countries have made public different indicators that help quantify the magnitude of illicit trade. In Indonesia, for instance, wildlife trafficking is described as being a market of wildlife valued at around $1 billion USD annually, within the country alone.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E (  It is also essential to emphasise that the scale of legal wildlife commerce can create conditions for laundering and enforcement overload. National reports found that 2.9 billion live animals were imported into the United States in just two decades, while the legal global wildlife trade is described as valued at $220 billion USD. Along with China, major consumer regions identified include the U.S., EU, and Japan, demonstrating how high-volume legal demand can place constant pressure on regulation systems and complicate efforts to distinguish legal trade from illegal trafficking.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
596 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
597 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E ( The scale of regulation itself also illustrates why traceability is challenging in practice. CITES keeps varying degrees of protection to over 40,900 species \(thousands of animal species and tens of thousands of plant species\). It is implemented by 185 Parties, meaning that traceability and documentation requirements must function across a very large number of species and jurisdictions, increasing both administrative burden and the risk of legal gaps that illegal actors can exploit.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
598 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 396 0 R
/T (International Measures and Efforts)
/E (International Measures and Efforts)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
599 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E ( All the previously presented data and background converge in one key fact: actions to tackle wildlife trade and ensure traceability rely on international, coordinated policy and enforcement. This primarily refers to having shared international standards for controlling trade and protecting biodiversity. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
600 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 396 0 R
/E ( For instance, Kenya has been shown to be a strong advocate for strict protections for elephants and other species at CITES. Furthermore, this country has collaborated with neighbouring countries on cross-border law enforcement and sharing information to dismantle entire trafficking networks. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
