/P 397 0 R
/E (Other entities and countries, such as the U.S, have placed regulations that address this issue, despite not being in direct collaboration with other countries. A clear example of this, is this country's Lacey Act, which restricts imports of illegally sourced wildlife or wood and supports regional anti-trafficking efforts.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 397 0 R
/E ( The U.S., however, has also been shown to direct several international operations; agencies including the U.S. Fish & Wildlife Service and Homeland Security routinely make large seizures and maintain special representatives working abroad. It is also noted that in 2016 the U.S. essentially banned domestic ivory sales, and that American tech companies cooperate in efforts to shut down online wildlife markets, reflecting the increasing importance of digital enforcement strategies. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 397 0 R
/E ( Another example of coordinated policy at scale is the European Union, which has treated wildlife trafficking as a cross-border priority requiring action among member states. The EU adopted an Action Plan against Wildlife Trafficking in 2016 and later introduced a revised action plan intended to strengthen ambition and serve as a blueprint for cooperation, within the EU and internationally, to reduce illegal wildlife trade. This kind of regional framework supports traceability goals by encouraging more consistent enforcement standards, stronger cooperation between authorities, and a shared approach to preventing illegal products from entering legal markets.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
609 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 397 0 R
/T (Current Situation)
/E (Current Situation)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 397 0 R
/E ( Current wildlife trade dynamics are increasingly shaped by the interaction between high-demand consumer markets and adaptable trafficking networks. Multiple countries, such as the United States, take the role as both major consumers and major enforcers. These countries remain as a large legal market for wildlife and wildlife products, while also serving as a destination for illegally sourced items such as ivory, reptile skins, and illegally harvested timber. This dual role complicates regulation, because high-volume legal flows can create opportunities for laundering and overwhelm monitoring systems.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
