617 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 398 0 R
/E (  Wildlife trade regulation and traceability are urgent because unregulated extraction and commerce represent a direct threat not only to individual species, but also to ecosystem stability and human well-being. Unregulated wildlife trade has been repeatedly shown to be able to disrupt ecological balance and undermine the natural systems that support climate stability. It is also described as being driven by short-term financial gains that can degrade biodiversity and harm the communities that rely on wildlife for sustainable livelihoods.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
618 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 398 0 R
/E ( In practice, the consequences of weak traceability are not limited to traditional wildlife products; high-value biological resources can become drivers of persistent exploitation when governance is fragile. Madagascar is used as an example of how poverty and weak enforcement can intensify illegal extraction: rare species and resources can become a concrete source of income in a very poor country, reinforcing the cycle of biodiversity loss. Initiatives such as community forestry and ecotourism are being pursued as more sustainable alternatives, highlighting that reducing illegal trade pressure often requires economic pathways that do not depend on trafficking.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
619 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 398 0 R
/E ( Ultimately, strengthening regulation and traceability is not simply a technical upgrade, it is a necessary condition for making conservation enforceable in real markets. Without transparent supply chains and credible verification, illegal products can continue to move through loopholes, undermining both biodiversity protection and the long-term economic security of communities that depend on legal and sustainable use. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
