714 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 404 0 R
/T (Historical Context and Background)
/E (Historical Context and Background)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
715 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 404 0 R
/E (The relationship between human societies and genetic diversity dates back more than 10,000 years, around 8000 BCE, when early agricultural communities began the domestication of plants and animals.. Indigenous peoples and traditional farming communities played a crucial role in conserving seeds, animal breeds, and ecological knowledge, establishing the foundations of food security and ecosystem resilience long before the emergence of modern states.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
716 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 404 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
717 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 404 0 R
/E (The trend accelerated further during the mid-20th century, particularly between the 1940s and 1970s, with the advent of the Green Revolution. High-yield crop varieties, combined with chemical fertilizers, pesticides, and irrigation, transformed global food production and helped prevent widespread famine. However, this progress came at a significant cost: monoculture farming systems expanded rapidly, displacing thousands of traditional crop varieties and contributing to severe genetic erosion. As agricultural systems became more genetically uniform, their vulnerability to pests, diseases, and environmental stress increased.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
718 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 404 0 R
/E (By the 1970s, growing scientific evidence and public concern brought environmental degradation and biodiversity loss to the forefront of international debate. The 1972 United Nations Conference on the Human Environment in Stockholm marked a turning point, as it formally linked environmental protection with economic and social development. During this period, researchers increasingly emphasized that genetic diversity was essential not only for conservation, but also for long-term sustainability and human survival.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
719 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 404 0 R
/E (A major milestone was reached in 1992, when the international community adopted the Convention on Biological Diversity \(CBD\) at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro. The CBD fundamentally reshaped global biodiversity governance by recognizing the conservation of biological diversity as a shared responsibility and by affirming national sovereignty over genetic resources. It introduced key principles such as prior informed consent and fair and equitable benefit-sharing, addressing long-standing concerns over the exploitation of biological resources from biodiversity-rich countries.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
720 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 404 0 R
/E (Building on this framework, the International Treaty on Plant Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture, adopted in 2001, acknowledged the historical contributions of farmers to the development and conservation of crop diversity. The treaty established a multilateral system to facilitate access to plant genetic resources )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
