724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (essential for global food security while promoting benefit-sharing and cooperation among states.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (The adoption of the Nagoya Protocol in 2010, which entered into force in 2014, further strengthened the access and benefit-sharing regime under the CBD. It sought to prevent biopiracy and ensure that benefits derived from genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge were shared fairly with provider countries, as well as Indigenous peoples and local communities who have long acted as stewards of biodiversity.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
726 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (Since 2015, with the adoption of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Paris Agreement on climate change, genetic diversity has been increasingly recognized as a critical component of climate adaptation and ecosystem resilience. In the 2020s, rapid advances in biotechnology, genome sequencing, and the use of digital sequence information have introduced new ethical and legal challenges, as genetic data can now be accessed and utilized without direct physical access to biological material.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (Overall, the historical evolution of genetic diversity protection illustrates a gradual transition from unregulated exploitation toward structured international cooperation. This progression underscores the growing recognition that safeguarding genetic diversity and ensuring equitable access to genetic resources are essential for addressing the accelerating loss of ecological variety and for promoting long-term global sustainability.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
728 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 405 0 R
