/P 406 0 R
/E ( Importance of biological diversity for human security)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
743 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E ( More than 50% of modern drug therapy comes from natural sources, and about 75% of food crops depend on pollinators \(this is an example of associationism: pollinators are associated with a certain level of genetic diversity in plants\) for production.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
744 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E ( Nearly 35% of the planet's wetlands have been lost since 1970, ecosystems that are key to maintaining genetic resources and environmental services such as freshwater and habitat for biological diversity.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
745 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E (Loss of local animal breeds)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
746 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E ( Of the more than 7,000 local livestock breeds that exist in the world, approximately 29% are in danger of extinction, indicating a significant erosion of the animal genetic biodiversity that sustains global agricultural and food systems.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
747 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 406 0 R
/T (International Measures and Efforts)
/E (International Measures and Efforts)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
748 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E (  The international community has become aware of the fact that the accelerated loss of genetic and biological diversity jeopardizes ecological stability, food security, and sustainable development. It has therefore established various regulatory frameworks and multilateral cooperation mechanisms for the conservation of genetic resources and to guarantee equitable access to the benefits derived from their use.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
749 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E ( The main instrument in this area is also the Convention on Biological Diversity, which was adopted in 1992 within the framework of the United Nations and establishes three fundamental objectives: the conservation of biological diversity, the sustainable use of its components, and the fair and equitable sharing of the benefits arising from the use of genetic resources. Through the CBD, the States Parties recognize the national sovereignty of their genetic resources and, at the same time, recognize the need to cooperate for the development, protection, and responsible management of biological resources.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
750 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 406 0 R
/E ( In turn, the Nagoya Protocol, in force since 2014, is a binding legal framework that regulates access to genetic resources and the equitable sharing of benefits derived from their use. It aims to prevent biopiracy while strengthening the rights of indigenous and local communities by recognizing their historical role in the conservation of genetic diversity and associated traditional knowledge.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
