/P 408 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
766 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 408 0 R
/T (International conservation organizations, particularly the World Wide Fund for Nature \(WWF\) and the International Union for Conservation of Nature \(IUCN\), have contributed significantly to current efforts. WWF has consistently highlighted genetic diversity loss in its Living Planet Reports, published every two years, with recent editions emphasizing the link between genetic erosion, ecosystem collapse, and climate change. Meanwhile, IUCN integrates genetic considerations into its Red List assessments and supports conservation policies that recognize genetic diversity as a core component of species survival.)
/E (International conservation organizations, particularly the World Wide Fund for Nature \(WWF\) and the International Union for Conservation of Nature \(IUCN\), have contributed significantly to current efforts. WWF has consistently highlighted genetic diversity loss in its Living Planet Reports, published every two years, with recent editions emphasizing the link between genetic erosion, ecosystem collapse, and climate change. Meanwhile, IUCN integrates genetic considerations into its Red List assessments and supports conservation policies that recognize genetic diversity as a core component of species survival.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
767 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 408 0 R
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
768 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 408 0 R
/T (In conclusion, the current situation reflects both progress and persistent challenges. International organizations, treaties, and conferences have established a robust institutional architecture to address genetic diversity loss, particularly since 2022. However, accelerating ecological pressures, unresolved issues such as DSI, and persistent inequalities in access and benefit-sharing continue to limit the effectiveness of global action. Addressing these challenges will require sustained political commitment, enhanced international cooperation, and adequate financing to translate global agreements into meaningful outcomes on the ground.)
/E (In conclusion, the current situation reflects both progress and persistent challenges. International organizations, treaties, and conferences have established a robust institutional architecture to address genetic diversity loss, particularly since 2022. However, accelerating ecological pressures, unresolved issues such as DSI, and persistent inequalities in access and benefit-sharing continue to limit the effectiveness of global action. Addressing these challenges will require sustained political commitment, enhanced international cooperation, and adequate financing to translate global agreements into meaningful outcomes on the ground.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
