/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E ( Genetic diversity enables species to adapt to environmental changes, resist disease, and fulfill their substantive ecological functions; its erosion reduces the resilience of ecosystems to climate change, environmental degradation, and extreme events, putting ecosystems at risk of irreversible collapse. The urgency of the issue is also reinforced by its consequences for food security and the livelihoods of millions of people. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
775 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (The genetic homogenization of crops and the loss of local breeds increase the vulnerability of global food systems to pests, droughts, and climate crises, which disproportionately affect developing countries and rural communities in particular. Furthermore, the loss of genetic resources limits future opportunities for scientific research, drug development, and technological innovation, deepening inequalities between states and regions.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
776 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E ( From a global perspective, failure to act on the erosion of genetic diversity jeopardizes the achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals, particularly zero hunger, climate action, and life on land and in water ecosystems. Without coordinated action, conflicts over natural resources would increase, economic insecurity would rise, and, at the same time, the risk of weakening environmental governance on a global scale would increase. The protection of genetic diversity must be considered a shared responsibility and a prerequisite for ensuring ecological stability, human well-being, and long-term sustainable development.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
777 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 409 0 R
/T (Pillars)
/E (Pillars)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
